1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,500
How many strangers would you need in a room before a shared
birthday becomes more likely than not?

2
00:00:07,033 --> 00:00:09,067
A hundred? Two hundred?

3
00:00:09,867 --> 00:00:11,300
Try twenty-three.

4
00:00:11,967 --> 00:00:14,967
If that feels wrong, hold on to that feeling.

5
00:00:15,267 --> 00:00:18,733
It points to the exact mistake this puzzle exposes.

6
00:00:19,033 --> 00:00:22,500
You may be imagining someone matching your birthday.

7
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,867
But nobody has to match you.

8
00:00:25,367 --> 00:00:27,267
Any two people can match.

9
00:00:27,567 --> 00:00:32,167
And every person who enters creates more chances than the
one before.

10
00:00:32,467 --> 00:00:35,533
The second person creates one possible pair.

11
00:00:35,833 --> 00:00:37,667
The third adds two more.

12
00:00:37,967 --> 00:00:43,533
By the time twenty-three people are standing here, there are
253 pairs.

13
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:49,067
You were looking at a small crowd. The probability was
looking at a much bigger network.

14
00:00:49,367 --> 00:00:53,700
But counting pairs is only the clue. Here is the
calculation.

15
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,633
Instead of asking when birthdays collide,

16
00:00:56,733 --> 00:01:00,067
ask what it takes for every birthday to be different.

17
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:02,933
The first person can have any birthday.

18
00:01:03,233 --> 00:01:06,200
The second must avoid one occupied date.

19
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:11,533
That leaves 364 out of 365 days.

20
00:01:11,833 --> 00:01:16,167
If both birthdays are different, the third must avoid two
dates.

21
00:01:16,467 --> 00:01:19,633
Now 363 days remain.

22
00:01:19,933 --> 00:01:23,367
Keep multiplying those chances as people enter.

23
00:01:23,667 --> 00:01:30,633
At twenty-three people, the chance that every birthday is
different is about 49.3%.

24
00:01:30,933 --> 00:01:33,767
So at least one shared birthday is the rest:

25
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:36,267
about 50.7%.

26
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:41,033
For this classic calculation, assume independent birthdays,

27
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:46,200
equally likely across 365 days. Ignore leap day.

28
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:49,900
That does not promise a match in this particular room.

29
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:54,733
It means that across many such rooms, just over half would
have one.

30
00:01:55,033 --> 00:01:59,200
And with fifty people, the chance exceeds 97%.

31
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:03,133
The surprise was never how many birthdays fit in a year.

32
00:02:03,433 --> 00:02:06,300
It was how many comparisons fit in a room.

33
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:10,967
Your intuition counted people. The probability counted
pairs.

34
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:12,667
Smarter choices.

35
00:02:12,967 --> 00:02:14,400
A better future.

36
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:16,133
Follow for more.
